WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.490
Film Courage:&nbsp; What are some of the archetypes
in YA novels?&nbsp;

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:09.050
Not just in the protagonist, where I hear
she is somehow the special girl, she is different

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.290
from all the others and has some type of special
powers.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:16.860
Jennifer Brody:&nbsp; There are a lot of tropes
that you will see in YA, for better or worse.&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.180
And one of them was just what you were describing.&nbsp;

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:21.830
They are special in some way.&nbsp;

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.060
They have some special gift or ability.&nbsp;

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.949
Or sometimes you see where they live in a
world where everyone has abilities and they

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:29.050
are the ones that don’t.&nbsp;

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:34.190
There is usually something that makes them
stand out in their world in some capacity.&nbsp;

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:40.749
Maybe you see that somewhat more in the Science
Fiction/Fantasy genre of YA.&nbsp;

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:42.789
But, yes, that is a classic trope.

00:00:42.789 --> 00:00:44.989
The Love Triangle is another one.&nbsp;

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:51.799
And I always think that people say “Oh,
this is Young Adult”&nbsp; but it’s also classic

00:00:51.799 --> 00:00:53.579
storytelling.&nbsp;

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:54.670
Romeo and Juliet.&nbsp;

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.499
You see this 400 million times.&nbsp;

00:00:56.499 --> 00:01:01.859
Romeo and Juliet has been told and retold
and was probably taken from something even

00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:05.039
before Shakespeare wrote Romeo and Juliet.&nbsp;

00:01:05.039 --> 00:01:07.340
So I think you see these things repeat a lot.&nbsp;

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:16.399
Romeo and Juliet does pop up a lot in YA….Star-Crossed
Lovers…Twilight is basically Romeo and Juliet

00:01:16.399 --> 00:01:17.399
with vampires.&nbsp;

00:01:17.399 --> 00:01:23.320
There is a book that is called (I think) Warm
Bodies that is Romeo and Juliet with zombies.&nbsp;

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.229
It was made into a film, as well.

00:01:27.229 --> 00:01:30.450
A lot of times you’ll see some of these
are coming-of-age stories.&nbsp;

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.859
Usually these characters are being oppressed
in some way.&nbsp;

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:35.610
It could be by parents or adults that rule
the world.&nbsp;

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:42.220
It could be by some greater antagonist but
usually they are fighting the system or something

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:43.700
against their world that isn’t fair.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.229
Film Courage:&nbsp; Let’s talk about other archetypes.&nbsp;

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:48.390
So you have the Jock character.&nbsp;

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:55.270
The Hot Jock who maybe seems like the dream
guy and then you have the Loner Guy that she

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.690
(the YA protagonist) is not supposed to be
with because her friends don’t approve but

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:58.999
she really likes.&nbsp;

00:01:58.999 --> 00:01:59.999
The Frenemy.&nbsp;

00:01:59.999 --> 00:02:04.590
The Nerdy Girl that is the side-kick, can
we talk about some of those archetypes?

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:05.920
Jennifer Brody:&nbsp; Yeah, that’s pretty typical.&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.310
Hermione from Harry Potter is your classic
version of that.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:15.450
In terms of Jock and Nerdy Guy, yeah…..you
are definitely still going to see that.&nbsp;

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.340
On the Netflix show Stranger Things which
is really fantastic, it does this 100%.&nbsp;

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:25.630
They do a few things to subvert that….I
think it is interesting to do turns with some

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:29.720
of those Jocky characters where they see the
light and come around a little.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:38.280
High school and the hierarchy of school, that
is going to be prevalent across all these

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.470
books, including the ones that aren’t even
in our world like Harry Potter, which may

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.430
skew younger to start but moves into YA territory.&nbsp;

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.870
Or even things like Twilight.&nbsp;

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:51.540
There is always a high school element in the
hierarchy of the society.&nbsp;

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:56.740
So you see all of that, as well.&nbsp;

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.690
I think it is fun to read those things.&nbsp;

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.720
Again, they are coming from somewhere which
is the real world.&nbsp;

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.310
If we’ve all been to high school, I feel
like we’ve all had these experiences.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.330
Film Courage:&nbsp; Right.&nbsp;

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:11.690
So it sounds like there is either the decision
to leave The Group or the decision to join

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.050
The Group and will they accept them [The Protagonist]?&nbsp;

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:17.050
They reverse in one of those two ways.&nbsp;

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:19.310
And is an adult always an oppressor?

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.440
Jennifer Brody:&nbsp; No, not always.&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:28.010
You will usually have characters that are
like nice, good adult characters or at least

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.410
I always try to write them into my books.&nbsp;

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:37.440
One of the things I did in my series is that
I did give point of views to adult characters.&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.110
That was one of the things I think that made
the book a challenge (right off the bat) to

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:46.630
sell even though we found a great publisher
and we did get a great book deal out of it.&nbsp;

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:52.120
But in the beginning, I got feedback like
“You have adult points of view in this book.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.600
How is this YA?”&nbsp; and I responded “Well,
the main characters are teens, but yes….there

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:58.120
are adults that live in this world.”&nbsp;

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:01.140
And I wanted to show some of that perspective.&nbsp;

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.230
Some of the adults are the antagonists.&nbsp;

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:09.540
In the case of the first book, it’s the
leader of the religious contingent who kind

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.360
of governs their world and gets some chapters
where we get to see their thought process,

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.020
to see what is happening on their end of the
story.&nbsp;

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:22.710
And otherwise we are going between Myra who
is a 16-year-old engineering apprentice in

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:29.130
the underwater world in an outer space colony
where our main character is and Aero, who

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:32.020
is a 16-year-old soldier in their military
society.&nbsp;

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.560
And so the story is told back and forth between
them.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:39.650
As the book series progresses, my second book
comes out in November [2016].&nbsp;

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:43.590
The story opens up a lot and we add more points
of view, so we start to track more characters

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.570
and some of those are adults.&nbsp;

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:51.900
We pick up an underwater character who is
an adult character and he carries through.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.370
I think it is fun to write a variety of different
perspectives.&nbsp;

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:59.120
Some of the adult characters (I hope) are
going to be positive, too.&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.900
But you will&nbsp; hopefully see it more focused
on the kids and teen characters.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.570
Question:&nbsp; What new character have you fallen
in love with this year?

